<!--hvy: {"lock":false,"expanded":true,"highlight":false}-->
#! Unnamed Section

 <!--hvy:text {"align":"center"}-->
  # SEPA Recreation Report
  
  ## Petrovitsky Park Disc Golf Course
  
  ### Prepared by James Hutchison
  
  Computer Scientist
<!--hvy: {"lock":false,"expanded":true,"highlight":false}-->
#! Abstract

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  ## Abstract

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  The proposed Petrovitsky Park disc golf course would have an adverse effect on alternative recreational uses of the
  property. It is currently undeveloped, with a small trail cutting through it, with potential for other improvements
  besides disc golf.
  
  The proposed disc golf course would monopolize the property due to its size relative to the usable space, and from the
  risks of thrown discs. Additionally, current trail users would be negatively impacted, because most walk the trail to
  enjoy a natural experience. Disc golf creates noise due to talking / yelling and would result in artificial clearings
  and paths for fairways, tee pads, holes, and players chasing discs. Discs make noise when they hit trees, enter brush,
  and hit a basket. Some of the proposed mitigations by King County Parks & Rec would further deviate from a natural
  experience, such as the proposal to frequently add mulch to fairways.
  
  A survey with 2 alternative variants was conducted in 8 of the neighborhoods surrounding the park. The primary survey
  received responses from 388 households and 481 individuals. Across all three variants, 546 responses were collected.
  There is an overwhelmingly negative response to the course. This is backed by both directly asking if the respondent
  supports the course or not, and indirectly from questions designed to gauge the motivations of the responder. The
  survey targeted open space, trail, and disc golf interests in the surrounding area. A majority of trail users would
  find the disc golf course detrimental to their experience.
  
  The trail is used by walkers, bicyclists, eBike riders, personal locomotion users, parents with strollers, dog
  walkers, elderly, and individuals who have challenges hiking but still enjoy nature and can walk or use an assistive
  device. A survey targeting disc golf would omit this class of individuals from the results, as they would have no
  interest in it. The trail provides a natural experience with minimal slope. The neighboring trail at McGarvey Park is
  much too steep, too challenging, and too far away for all of these users.
  
  There is an opportunity for the county to spend greater effort making the trail feel more natural. There is also an
  opportunity for improvements along the border with the neighboring baseball fields, although it would be prudent to
  measure community support before implementing anything.
<!--hvy: {"lock":false,"expanded":true,"highlight":false}-->
#! Community Survey

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  ## Independent Community Survey

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  A survey was conducted amongst the neighborhoods near Petrovitsky Park. A 13 question survey asked respondants about
  their trail use, about potential improvements, and finally about the disc golf course. The survey was conducted as an
  informed survey, meaning respondants were given both sides to the argument for the disc golf course, as well as
  contract information, mitigations, and mitigation rebuttal. Homes were given a document with a unique access code.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  The survey intended to target trail users, disc golfer enthusiasts, and anyone who may have interest in answering a
  survey about uses for the open space.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  The survey methodology and analysis is presented later in this document.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  The survey revealed that a large majority of respondents (83-84%) have used the forest trail before. A few were
  unaware there was a trail (7.5-7.9%). 87.5% stated they would use the trail.
  
  This disc golf course design was widely not supported amongst survey respondents. About 72.6-75.3% of respondents were
  opposed to the disc golf course. Of those opposed, 80.1-82.9% were strongly opposed.
  
  The survey focused on this specific 27 hole design, and not on disc golf in general. In the analysis section we can
  attempt to predict how many individuals may be swayed to a smaller design, as well as predict how the survey may
  perform over a larger area.
<!--hvy: {"lock":false,"expanded":true,"highlight":false}-->
#! Current Uses

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  ## Current Uses

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  The trail is currently used by a wide range of individuals, usually walking or bicycling. The trail is also frequently
  used by individuals who walk their dogs. The Petrovitsky Park trail is unique due to its accessibility. It is located
  a short walk from the play area and has a fairly flat incline across two of the three entrances.
  
  Activity depends on the time of day. Anecdotally, late afternoon / evening hours typically see the most use.
  
  A large majority (82.9-83.8%) of survey respondents have used the forest trail before. A large majority (87.5%) also
  currently use or would use the trail.
  
  One of the highlights of the trail is the southern entrance, near the soccer fields, which very quickly reveals a
  forest filled with moss and ferns, offering a quality northwestern forest experience with minimal walking. At sunset,
  during the summer, this area creates a photographic opportunity due to the golden sunlight reaching into the forest.
  No other portion of the trail provides this benefit. 73.9-75.5% of respondents agreed this area should be preserved
  and kept completely natural. 60.6-61.5% felt that the area should have walking limited to minimize damage to the
  ferns.

 <!--hvy:image {"imageFile":"Screenshot 2026-08-07 at 2.47.37â€¯PM.png","imageAlt":"Panorama of forest and trail"}-->

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  84.8% felt the park is not doing enough to keep the trail experience feeling natural. Examples include the dumping of
  trimmings and branches next to the trail and cutting the plants adjacent to the trail. 91.3-92.2% of respondents
  stated that they would walk the trail to enjoy a nature escape.
<!--hvy: {"lock":false,"expanded":true,"highlight":false}-->
#! Alternative Uses

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  ## Alternative Uses

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  Several categories of alternative uses were considered for this report. This includes "sacrificing" a portion of the
  trail that wasn't particularly unique for an alternative use, leveraging already disturbed areas, improvements against
  the forest boundary, and ways to increase "forest time" by adding a destination.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  48.2-48.7% of respondents would at least occasionally use a park bench if it was installed in the forest, next to the
  trail. 10.2-10.6% stated they would use it frequently.
  
  Attitudes towards leveraging an already disturbed area and minor improvements along the forest edge were mixed, with a
  slight skew towards support. This suggests that there's opportunity, but Not Sure, and Not Sure / Depends have a
  strong showing for these questions.
  
  While environmental concerns are one of the top reasons given in the feedback portion of the survey, one key takeaway
  is that respondents aren't universally against any sort of development.
<!--hvy: {"lock":false,"expanded":true,"highlight":false}-->
#! Disc Golf Support

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  ## Disc Golf Support

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  72.6-75.3% of respondents do not support the disc golf course, with a heavy skew on "strongly do not support"
  (58.1-62.4%). 
  
  Unfortunately, King County Parks & Rec did not provide a "minimal viable design" for people to vote on, so support for
  a less ambitious design could not be directly measured.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  One important question is whether support would change with other disc golf designs.
  
  A possible interpretation of this data is that "support" and "do not support" suggest the responder sees potentially
  reconcilable flaws in the design while "strongly do not support" suggests the responder does not see a satisfactory
  design as possible.
  
  However, bias measurements done via alternative surveys weakly suggest that "strongly not support" is influenced by
  how overwhelming the evidence against the course is.

 <!--hvy:image {"css":"margin: 0.5rem auto; width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; size: 0.75rem;","imageFile":"Screenshot 2026-08-26 at 4.54.14â€¯PM.png","imageAlt":"Screenshot 2026-08-26 at 4.54.14â€¯PM.png","caption":{"text":"As the survey tilts the bias towards the \"for\" side, a _slight_ trend emerges where \"strongly do not support\" moves towards \"do not support\"","schema":{"kind":"text","id":"","component":"text","editorOnly":false,"lock":false,"align":"center","slot":"center","css":"margin: 0.5rem 0;","sortKeys":{},"derivedSortKeyNames":[],"groupKeys":{},"tags":"","description":"","hideIfYes":"","visibleScript":"","placeholder":"","fillIn":false,"showCopy":false,"metaOpen":false,"xrefTitle":"","xrefDetail":""}}}-->
