#! Nearby Recreation

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  ## Nearby Recreation

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  Fairwood is near several actual golf courses. At the intersection of 140th and Maple Valley Highway is a public
  course, and off of 140th is a private golf course.
  
  There's also a public, free, 18 hole disc golf course a 10 - 15 minute drive away, in Maple Valley at Gaffney's Grove
  (Lake Wilderness Park).
  
  Other drivable disc golf courses surrounding Petrovitsky Park: Lake Fenwick, White River, SeaTac, Ralph
  Williamson/Lakewood, Fort Steilacoom Northwest, Fort Steilacoom Rhododendron, Juel Park, North Park (Mineral Springs)
  
  Petrovitsky Park is home to 2 full size soccer fields and four baseball fields, as well as a play area. A splash pad
  is slated to be installed in the near future.
  
  Lake Desire provides a public fishing, boating, and swimming opportunities, although the dock is currently undersized
  and in disrepair.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  ### McGarvey Park Permanent Open Space Trail
  
  There is also a trail near the Petrovitsky Park in the McGarvey Park Permanent Open Space. The portions near
  Petrovitsky Park are substantially less friendly towards the young child / bike / elderly / stroller / mobility issue
  demographic due to the steepness and narrowness of the trail. The public entrance near Lake Desire is especially
  steep. The non-public Woodside entrance also contains large steps. The McGarvey Park permanent open space trail should
  not be considered a substitute for the Petrovitsky Park trail.

 <!--hvy:image {"css":"margin: 0.5rem auto; display: block; size: 0.75rem;","imageFile":"Screenshot 2026-08-26 at 9.58.35â€¯AM.png","imageAlt":"Screenshot 2026-08-26 at 9.58.35â€¯AM.png","caption":{"text":"The McGarvey Park Trail is lacking appropriate signage, as the sign is unreadable and it is adjacent to private property.","schema":{"kind":"text","id":"","component":"text","editorOnly":false,"lock":false,"align":"center","slot":"center","css":"margin: 0.5rem 0;","sortKeys":{},"derivedSortKeyNames":[],"groupKeys":{},"tags":"","description":"","hideIfYes":"","visibleScript":"","placeholder":"","fillIn":false,"showCopy":false,"metaOpen":false,"xrefTitle":"","xrefDetail":""}}}-->
<!--hvy: {"lock":false,"expanded":true,"highlight":false}-->
#! Recreational Displacement

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  ## Recreational Displacement

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  The proposed disc golf course will displace / harm the enjoyment of trail users. This is because of numerous reasons:
  
  - The design will extensively clear foliage visible from the trail, detracting from a natural appearance.
  - Trail users enjoy being in nature, and disc golfers shouting, cheering, and talking disrupt from that.
  - The sparse usage encourages wildlife sightings. Increased, noisier human activity will scare wildlife away more frequently.
  - The design violates good design principals and encourages throwing at the trail with insufficient margins, creating risks to trail users
  - The design will inject 5 holes and 5 tee pads in the photogenic natural area referred to earlier (the gold "Desktop Background" in the image below). 73.9%-75.5% of respondents stated this should be kept completely natural, and 60.6%-61.5% felt walking through the ferns should be minimized.
  - Active recreational usage by the disc golfers will be exasperated by others taking less care to stay on the trail, creating more trails and sprawl. Large predators such as bears will use these trails, further increasing trample of the understory. Signage is always inevitably ignored by at least a few people and will be insufficient. It is unrealistic to believe that disc golfers will abandon their mis-thrown discs and let the NWDGA recover it for them. This is literally the opposite of what disc golfers have stated will happen in online discussion when the littering of discs was brought up.
  
  Increased human usage will likely negatively impact wildlife sightings in the area, which extends beyond just the
  park. Petrovitsky Park's forest is home to many different animals, and visited by deer, opossum, raccoons, eagles,
  osprey, bear, and more.
  
  59.6-61.9% of survey respondents stated that enjoyment of the trail would be harmed by visible clearing of the
  understory. All other suggestions, such as adding a park bench, leveraging the disturbed area, etc. are mutually
  incompatible with an environment where disc golfers are throwing discs from potentially any direction.
  
  Additionally, alternative uses proposed in the survey near the softball field edge would be pushed out due to the
  flying disc risk. Some alternative uses could include picnic areas, a 9 hole outdoor mini-golf course, another play
  set, swings, and other amenities. This particular disc golf course design would interfere, primarily due to the holes
  on the west edge of the forest. 57.4-58.6% of respondents would support converting the disturbed area on the west side
  of the forest where the park has been dumping materials into small footprint amenity. 59.5-59.7% of respondents would
  support minor improvements that minimally disturb the forest along the softball field edge.

 <!--hvy:image {"css":"margin: 0.5rem auto; width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; size: 0.75rem;","imageFile":"image006.png","imageAlt":"image006.png","caption":{"text":"Bottom red area - requested 100 meter buffer from Trovitsky Park properties\n\nGold area - Fern covered area that is accessible by sunlight at sunset\n\nOrange areas - Visibility from trail / foliage buffer","schema":{"kind":"text","id":"","component":"text","editorOnly":false,"lock":false,"align":"center","slot":"center","css":"margin: 0.5rem 0;","sortKeys":{},"derivedSortKeyNames":[],"groupKeys":{},"tags":"","description":"","hideIfYes":"","visibleScript":"","placeholder":"","fillIn":false,"showCopy":false,"metaOpen":false,"xrefTitle":"","xrefDetail":""}}}-->

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  ### Responses that conflict with this design (Primary Survey)

 <!--hvy:table {"tableColumns":["Response","Percentage"],"tableColumnProperties":{"Response":{"wrap":true,"truncate":false}}}-->
  | Response | Percentage |
  | --- | --- |
  | I (would) walk the trail to enjoy a nature escape | 91.3 - 92.2% |
  | The park should make greater efforts to keep the trail experience feeling natural | 84.8% |
  | Should efforts be made to preserve this as a completely natural photograph opportunity? | 73.9 - 75.5% |
  | Do you support moving forward with this disc golf course? | 72.6 - 75.3% |
  | Do you feel walking through these ferns should be avoided to minimize damage to the fronds? | 60.6 - 61.5% |
  | Would you support minor improvements that minimally disturb the forest along this area | 59.5 - 59.7% |
  | I would support converting the disturbed area into a minor amenity such as a picnic table, small footprint play area, or similar | 56.4 - 58.6% |
  | I would use a park bench if installed in the forest, next to the trail | 48.2 - 48.7% (occasionally or more frequent) |
  | I would enjoy the trail less if portions of the forest understory (brush, ferns, etc.) were visibly thinned or cleared | 59.6 - 61.9% |
<!--hvy: {"lock":false,"expanded":true,"highlight":false}-->
#! Mitigations

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  ## Mitigations

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  The top mitigation, if 18 holes is truly a _requirement_, is to simply look elsewhere. This is a sensitive area, and
  there's already plenty of disc golf options in the area and there is simply no need for one here. A disc golf course
  is a benefit to users of it, and a nuisance to those who do not use it. The course would impose an opportunity cost as
  other amenities (e.g. swings, picnic area, etc) are now no longer possible.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  The next mitigation is to reduce the design to 9 holes and eliminate the beginner's course. Referring to the picture
  above, there's room for 9 holes within the uncolored regions of the map, and some minor intrusion on the 100 meter
  buffer is likely acceptable. This would avoid homes, avoid the trail, could be laid out to give a predicable movement
  flow, and avoid the golden area. It would also substantially reduce the natural impact by being smaller footprint and
  downslope, be less compelling by not being tournament quality, so less likely to create dense traffic issues, but
  would still give individuals a disc golf experience. For many, 18 holes is too many to do in a single sitting anyways.
  The redundant tees and holes could be removed to minimize the footprint of the course. One of the concerns brought up
  is that NWDGA's volunteers may be unqualified to manage this land, and if that ends up being true, the damage is
  mitigated. If they are qualified, then this is an opportunity to prove themselves.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  Concerns about the disc golf course inviting drug use in the proximity of an elementary school and middle school are
  worth addressing. The trail is also well suited for young children, strollers, and older individuals with mobility
  limitations who would prefer the flatter, shorter trail to any sort of hiking trail. Designating Petrovitsky Park and
  the natural space as a drug free zone and implementing stiff penalties for violators would help with that.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  Trail usage could be increased with greater efforts to make it compelling while also helping others know it exists.
  The trail entrances have no signage leading to them (7.5-7.9% were unaware the trail existed, although a portion may
  have also never visited the park). The trail has no destination (such as a bench or picnic table) where one can simply
  sit and enjoy the tranquility of the forest. The trail is low slope, but the T section is somewhat steep leading
  downhill, and may be too steep for individuals with mobility issues and requires a bit of braking on a bicycle or
  similar device. Many of the older individuals with mobility issues, who would appreciate the trail, are unaware it
  exists.
  
  84.8% of respondents feel the park should make _greater_ efforts to keep the trail experience feeling natural. A lot
  of things have been dumped along the trail and trimming the neighboring foliage seems to fundamentally misunderstand
  why people use the trail in the first place. Since 17.8-19% of respondents support the disc golf course, there's clear
  overlap where even some disc golf supporters want things to stay natural as well.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  Another mitigation, which may not be possible, is to allow the growth around the trail to grow extensively like a
  natural fence and have very few entrances / exits on the disc golf course. This would help keep a natural appearance,
  insulate noise, and minimize trail user disturbance.
<!--hvy: {"lock":false,"expanded":true,"highlight":false}-->
#! Survey Methodology

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  # Independent Survey Methodology

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  A thirteen question informed survey was created to evaluate attitude towards the forest and confirm potential
  alternative uses. The goal of the informed survey is that one is answering it, neutrally, with all the facts at hand,
  much in the same way one may vote for a ballot proposal. The intent was to validate whether potential areas of
  protection or use were plausibly worth considering. The fern area (gold area on the map above) and whether individuals
  felt it was worth preserving in a natural state is one example. The county has described the disc golf course as using
  "natural clearings", but no such things actually exist in the park. An alternative interpretation is that the fern
  area is considered a "natural clearing", because there's primarily large trees and not extensive foliage.
  
  The survey was piloted at the Fairwood Market with business owners who live outside the area, then adjusted. The
  measured response rate at the market was 10%. Some of the questions were rephrased to try to reduce drop-out from
  perceived bias. For example, one of the original questions asked if "The undeveloped forest and trail at Petrovitsky
  Park is special (agree / disagree)," however it was viewed as ambiguous and high friction. Likewise, the piloted
  survey was missing "Not sure" answers, and it was viewed that if someone couldn't provide their opinion accurately
  they may drop out of the survey.

 <!--hvy:table {"tableColumns":["Question","Purpose","Answer May Be Incompatible w/ Disc Golf Course"],"tableColumnProperties":{"Question":{"wrap":true,"truncate":false},"Purpose":{"wrap":true,"truncate":false},"Answer May Be Incompatible w/ Disc Golf Course":{"wrap":true,"truncate":false}}}-->
  | Question | Purpose | Answer May Be Incompatible w/ Disc Golf Course |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | Have you used the forest trail in Petrovitsky Park? | Understand whether the individual has seen the forest and trail before giving an opinion. Also to measure how many are even aware of the trail. | No |
  | The undeveloped forest and trail at Petrovitsky Park is worth preserving (agree / disagree) | Understand whether the individual finds value, sees it as just woods, underutilized property, etc. | No |
  | I currently use or would use the trail | Differentiate trail users from those that have at least some familiarity with the property | No |
  | I would enjoy the trail less if portions of the forest understory (brush, ferns, etc) were visibly thinned or cleared | Understand whether the individual values the natural experience over simply having a place to walk | This design yes, but tolerance is unstated and will vary |
  | Should efforts be made to preserve this as a completely natural photograph opportunity? | Understand if others view this as worth keeping pristine or not | This design yes, several holes cut through it |
  | Do you feel walking through these ferns should be avoided to minimize damage to the fronds? | Understand if keeping pristine is worth explicitly segmenting off | This design yes, several holes cut through it |
  | I (would) walk the trail to enjoy a nature escape | Understand if the objective of walking the trail is to be isolated, alone, in nature | Yes as no course on this property could be sufficiently far enough away. A design that affected less of the trail would mitigate this |
  | I would use a park bench if installed in the forest, next to the trail | Evaluate whether a bench next to the trail is worth doing | This design encumbers the trail outside the wetlands buffer, so yes. Inside the buffer there is room sufficiently away from the course on the eastern leg of the trail. |
  | The park should make greater efforts to keep the trail experience feeling natural | Evaluate whether others have noticed the accumulation of debris / prioritize natural experience | This design yes |
  | I would support converting the disturbed area into a minor amenity such as picnic table, small footprint play area, or similar | Evaluate whether others would support some development in an already disturbed place | This design yes (hole 2) |
  | Would you support minor improvements that minimally disturb the forest along this area? | Evaluate whether undisturbed but minor improvements are supported | This design yes (hole 2) |
  | Have you already heard the arguments for / against the disc golf course in Petrovitsky Park? | Determine whether the answer to the next question is affected by anything on the survey or if the individual likely already had an opinion | No |
  | Do you support moving forward with this disc golf course? | Evaluate whether they support this disc golf course design based on facts provided | This design yes |

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  Survey notices with unique access codes were hand delivered to front doors, usually taped to the door. The access code
  was associated with the neighborhood in question. Each home received its own unique access code.

 <!--hvy:image {"css":"margin: 0.5rem auto; width: 40rem; height: auto; display: block; size: 0.75rem;","imageFile":"Screenshot 2026-08-24 at 1.14.24â€¯PM.png","imageAlt":"Screenshot 2026-08-24 at 1.14.24â€¯PM.png","caption":{"text":"\"Example\" is the name of the neighborhood. Replace it with \"Lake Desire\", etc.","schema":{"kind":"text","id":"","component":"text","editorOnly":false,"lock":false,"align":"center","slot":"center","css":"margin: 0.5rem 0;","sortKeys":{},"derivedSortKeyNames":[],"groupKeys":{},"tags":"","description":"","hideIfYes":"","visibleScript":"","placeholder":"","fillIn":false,"showCopy":false,"metaOpen":false,"xrefTitle":"","xrefDetail":""}}}-->

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  The survey and access code was written using custom software, committed to a private GitHub repository, and hosted on
  Google Cloud Run. The domain "petroparksurvey.org" was chosen for hosting the survey.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  ### Neighborhoods

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  The final neighborhood coverage was not known at the start. It was dependent on the amount of resources that could be
  directed and time constraints. Several neighborhoods had HOA or newsletters discuss the course and recommend a stance
  for individuals already.

 <!--hvy:table {"tableColumns":["Neighborhood","Formally Opposed"],"tableColumnProperties":{"Formally Opposed":{"align":"center"}}}-->
  | Neighborhood | Formally Opposed |
  | --- | --- |
  | Trovitsky Park | Yes |
  | Lake Desire | No |
  | Woodside | Yes |
  | The Parks | No |
  | Fairwood Greens | No |
  | Carriage Wood | No |
  | Candlewood Ridge | No |

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  The borders of Candlewood Ridge and Fairwood Greens were difficult to isolate and an error was made while distributing
  the survey. The website nextdoor.com provided borders for neighborhoods but it was later determined to be incorrect,
  encompassing Candlewood Ridge as part of Fairwood Greens. A new neighborhood was invented ("Near Ridgewood") to
  represent the portion of Candlewood Ridge that would possibly also include Fairwood Greens. The erroneous layout
  already had the edges of Candlewood Ridge delivered to, so the interior portion was safely Candlewood Ridge. This
  resulted in Fairwood Greens getting at least a handful of survey responses attributed to it when they should have gone
  to another neighborhood.
  
  Finally, Fairwood Greens is large, so the southeast portion (closest to Petrovitsky Park) was serviced first, with the
  northeast and any other region being "as needed". By the point the southeast portion was completed, patterns had
  emerged across all the neighborhoods that hadn't substantially changed over time, indicating that serving to more
  homes was unlikely to shift the result by any meaningful amount.
  
  The courier was also very tired and time was running out to have the survey ready for this SEPA document.

 <!--hvy:image {"css":"margin: 0.5rem auto; width: 100%; height: auto; display: block;","imageFile":"Screenshot 2026-08-25 at 4.53.00â€¯PM.png","imageAlt":"Screenshot 2026-08-25 at 4.53.00â€¯PM.png"}-->

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  ### Responses by Neighborhood (All Survey Variants)

 <!--hvy:table {"tableColumns":["Neighborhood","Responses"]}-->
  | Neighborhood | Responses |
  | --- | --- |
  | Trovitsky Park | 59 |
  | Lake Desire | 41 |
  | Woodside | 148 |
  | The Parks | 62 |
  | Fairwood Greens (southeast)\* | 100 |
  | Carriage Wood | 85 |
  | Candlewood Ridge\* | 18 |
  | Near Ridgewood\* | 33 |
  | Total | 546 |

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  A response in this case is a "raw" response. Access codes can be reused to register multiple responses across
  different homeowners.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  ### Fraud Monitoring

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  Access code, IP address, and user agent were monitored for signs of "ballot stuffing". Only one IP address had more
  than one access code and it was likely a coincidence caused by IP address reuse. Some access codes had many
  submissions, with the greatest being 7, all "strongly against" the disc golf course, followed by 5 all being "strongly
  for" the disc golf course. Review of the submissions didn't suggest obvious fraud. Most duplicates on an access code
  were 2.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  It was noted that votes "for" the disc golf course were showing clustering but a cause is not known. One can speculate
  that individuals communicated between each other about the survey, likely causing the spikes.

 <!--hvy:image {"css":"margin: 0.5rem auto; width: 100%; height: auto; display: block;","imageFile":"Screenshot 2026-08-27 at 4.41.53â€¯PM.png","imageAlt":"Screenshot 2026-08-27 at 4.41.53â€¯PM.png","caption":{"text":"Screenshot of support over time as the survey was delivered","schema":{"kind":"text","id":"","component":"text","editorOnly":false,"lock":false,"align":"center","slot":"center","css":"margin: 0.5rem 0;","sortKeys":{},"derivedSortKeyNames":[],"groupKeys":{},"tags":"","description":"","hideIfYes":"","visibleScript":"","placeholder":"","fillIn":false,"showCopy":false,"metaOpen":false,"xrefTitle":"","xrefDetail":""}}}-->

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  ### Delivery Methodology

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  The courier was to deliver the notice to the front door of home. If it looked like someone is plausibly on vacation
  (no animals or noises, no cars out of front), and the front door is street facing, the notice placed out of view,
  unless no option was available.
  
  If the homeowner was outside or opened the door as the notice is being delivered, the notice was to be explained as,
  "A survey about the Petrovitsky Park open space and trail, as well as the proposed disc golf course." The courier was
  to avoid indicating a position.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  ### Bias Challenges, Mitigations, and Measurement

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  The survey had several challenges with attempting to look unbiased, especially in regards to the last question,
  whether the disc golf course is supported. For starters, the fact it wasn't from King County may already lead one to
  believe the survey was oppositionally lead. The next issue was that the SEPA related questions, especially question 5,
  needed to convey why something would be important, and something that's especially pretty at sunset is easily missed.
  This can create the appearance of a gauntlet of "pro nature" questions, and be interpreted as being obscenely biased
  to someone coming into the survey already ready to vote in favor of the disc golf course.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  Incidentally, the risk of biased _appearance_ is less that the _answers_ are biased but rather that one side drops out
  of the survey more frequently than another, creating biased _results_.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  Other challenges included the fact that King County Parks & Rec had no documentation describing the benefits of the
  project other than their mini-site and FAQ (this was confirmed by emailing Charlie Hohlbein, the project manager),
  prior to conducting the survey. Users would drop out of the survey if directed to another website, and had to dig
  through the site to find supporting arguments. This forced using artificial intelligence to summarize points from the
  website and information extracted from the contract and applications from a prior data pull.
  
  Another challenge was that the county's information about the environmental mitigations were misleading. For example,
  it claimed that natural clearing would be used when none existed. It listed square footage numbers of clearing that
  failed the sniff test or weren't consistent. This merited allowing the opposition to provide a rebuttal to the
  information. This structure intrinsically gives the opposition the last word, which further creates a biased
  appearance. Incidentally, you're also stuck with a dilemma - if one is allowed to misinform, then that is biased
  towards the misinformer. If the misinformer is corrected, that's biased towards the opposition. There's no neutral
  in-between.
  
  Another confounding matter was that the oppositional side of the argument was longer, which is often to be expected
  with a large project that has loose ends. Each insufficiently unsolved problem is essentially an argument against the
  project. You could trim them, but then which ones do you choose to trim?
  
  To help mitigate these issues on the survey, the environmental mitigations were placed in an expandable element and
  kept out of the for / against arguments.
  
  Another change was to randomize the order of the for / against, so the "last word" is randomized.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  As the survey progressed, some of the comments accused it of being biased without specifically calling anything out
  (this was from votes that "strongly favored" the disc golf course). At this point the remaining neighborhoods were
  Fairwood Greens, Carriage Wood, and Candlewood Ridge. Several potential sources of bias were identified, and there
  simply wasn't enough resources to do too many variations of the survey, so two additional surveys were created for the
  remaining neighborhoods:
  
  - A survey with the mitigations moved to the "pro" side of the disc golf arguments, the rebuttal removed entirely, references to "SavePetroPark.org" (i.e. opinion provided by SavePetroPark.org) replaced with "local coalition", and the "against" arguments trimmed to the main, most prominent points so that they were visually smaller than the "for" arguments
  - A survey with all the changes above but also removal of the SEPA related questions 5-11 (so only 6 total). This removes the fern area, which already had sufficient data.
  
  These surveys were delivered in a 2/1/1 ratio, with random clustering. This meant a set of homes in a row may receive
  the same survey variant. The two 13 question surveys were indistinguishable on the printed notice while the 6 question
  survey only mentioned having 6 questions. All surveys stated they took 5 - 10 minutes, even though the 6 question
  survey was obviously shorter.
<!--hvy: {"lock":false,"expanded":true,"highlight":false}-->
#! Analysis

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  # Survey Analysis and Opinion

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  The survey was the product of a tight deadline and limited resources. The response rate has blown away my
  expectations, suggesting that this is a hot button topic for many people that motivates them to look at a survey on
  their door from an unnamed stranger, possibly fear its a phishing attempt or something else, and answer it anyways. We
  easily blew past the 10% target rate. Mind you, we didn't tell anyone we were doing this. We had neighbors ask if we
  did it because they genuinely didn't know, and were wondering if there was another group involved.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  While this survey was about extracting SEPA information, there was, up to this point, no other survey that actually
  solicited the public on the final design. We needed all this information, but also had no idea how many people would
  respond. This means asking a set of questions that could influence the answer of the later ones.
  
  The big question is whether the results were skewed by bias. Having someone accuse the survey of bias doesn't actually
  mean the results are biased. If a survey _obviously appears_ biased, like it's trying to get you to vote a certain
  way, individuals with prior opinions are unlikely to change their minds. If someone comes in on the fence, they'll see
  the survey is biased and put up their guard. If anything, they'll be dismissive of the arguments they read and vote
  the opposite of what they otherwise would have.
  
  This was identified as a challenge, and ultimately it ended up working out.

 <!--hvy:image {"css":"margin: 0.5rem auto; width: 100%; height: auto; display: block;","imageFile":"Screenshot 2026-08-26 at 4.54.14â€¯PM.png","imageAlt":"Screenshot 2026-08-26 at 4.54.14â€¯PM.png","caption":{"text":"The shorthand survey, the most biased in favor of the disc golf course, has the _worst_ showing of \"strong support\"","schema":{"kind":"text","id":"","component":"text","editorOnly":false,"lock":false,"align":"center","slot":"center","css":"margin: 0.5rem 0;","sortKeys":{},"derivedSortKeyNames":[],"groupKeys":{},"tags":"","description":"","hideIfYes":"","visibleScript":"","placeholder":"","fillIn":false,"showCopy":false,"metaOpen":false,"xrefTitle":"","xrefDetail":""}}}-->

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  Notably, the shortened survey, the one with the least potential biases in favor of the opposition side, has fewer
  responses and the least that "strongly support" it. This suggests that the biased appearance actually motivated people
  to complete the survey when they would have otherwise dropped out. Likewise, the perceived bias of the 13 question
  surveys may have motivated people to vote against the opposition position to spite the survey. One comment suggests
  this happened at least once. Keep in mind, people have their guard up when they answer the survey. They may take all
  the oppositional arguments with _no_ grains of salt. The disc golf support may very well be _over-represented_.

 <!--hvy:image {"imageFile":"Screenshot 2026-08-26 at 9.05.51â€¯PM.png","imageAlt":"Screenshot 2026-08-26 at 9.05.51â€¯PM.png","caption":{"text":"\"Previously heard arguments\" is no","schema":{"kind":"text","id":"","component":"text","editorOnly":false,"lock":false,"align":"center","slot":"center","css":"margin: 0.5rem 0;","sortKeys":{},"derivedSortKeyNames":[],"groupKeys":{},"tags":"","description":"","hideIfYes":"","visibleScript":"","placeholder":"","fillIn":false,"showCopy":false,"metaOpen":false,"xrefTitle":"","xrefDetail":""}}}-->

 <!--hvy:image {"imageFile":"Screenshot 2026-08-26 at 9.09.46â€¯PM.png","imageAlt":"Screenshot 2026-08-26 at 9.09.46â€¯PM.png","caption":{"text":"\"Previously heard arguments\" is yes, so this should largely give an idea of the random variance due to the small sample size","schema":{"kind":"text","id":"","component":"text","editorOnly":false,"lock":false,"align":"center","slot":"center","css":"margin: 0.5rem 0;","sortKeys":{},"derivedSortKeyNames":[],"groupKeys":{},"tags":"","description":"","hideIfYes":"","visibleScript":"","placeholder":"","fillIn":false,"showCopy":false,"metaOpen":false,"xrefTitle":"","xrefDetail":""}}}-->

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  The dropout rate could have been measured, but with people able to reuse codes, possibly looking at the survey,
  deciding to do it later, then forgetting, etc. it wasn't clear if the dropout rate could carry any meaning, so
  monitoring it wasn't implemented. It would have very likely been incorrect for the neighborhoods that had less time to
  respond.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  It's worth reiterating that this is an informed survey, and the survey variants aren't going from "obviously biased in
  favor of the disc golf opposition to not biased", but rather we're shifting the bias towards "for the disc golf
  course" by progressively neutering the arguments against the disc golf course.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  ### Would people vote against a lesser course?

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  We can try to predict what percentage of the population is open to a lesser course. We'll have to assume our "for"
  disc golf votes aren't going to switch in protest that they didn't get the larger course.

 <!--hvy:table {"tableColumns":["Behavior","Count (weighted / raw)","Facet"],"tableColumnProperties":{"Facet":{"wrap":true}}}-->
  | Behavior | Count (weighted / raw) | Facet |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | Likely against all development | 49 / 55 (12.6% / 11.4%) | Don't support disc golf, minor improvements, disturbed area |
  | Against course but not development | 158 / 199 (40.6% / 41.4%) | Don't support disc golf, support minor improvements |
  | Support course | 74 / 86 (19.0% / 17.9%) | Support disc golf course |
  | Undecided | 33 / 33 (8.5% / 6.7%) | Not sure if support disc golf course |
  | Uncategorized | 75 / 108 (19.3% / 22.5%) | Don't support, neither of first 2 rows |
  | Total | 389 / 481 (100% / 100%) |  |

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  Based on this analysis, it would appear an alternative course isn't _ruled out_, but it would need to change a sizable
  amount of minds, which is difficult to do. Most individuals will begin their opinion opposing the course, and need to
  be convinced a lesser course is more reasonable. The uncategorized row is also a substantial size and based on their
  responses do not obviously support minor development.

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  ### What would the survey look like if it was expanded beyond Petrovitsky Park?

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  We can use Fairwood Greens (southeast) and Carriage Wood as a reference, as they have been most insulated from the
  arguments about the disc golf course:

 <!--hvy:grid {"gridColumns":3}-->
  <!--hvy:grid:0 {}-->

   <!--hvy:image {"imageFile":"Screenshot 2026-08-27 at 12.45.11â€¯PM.png","imageAlt":"Screenshot 2026-08-27 at 12.45.11â€¯PM.png","caption":{"text":"Carriage Wood","schema":{"kind":"text","id":"","component":"text","editorOnly":false,"lock":false,"align":"center","slot":"center","css":"margin: 0.5rem 0;","sortKeys":{},"derivedSortKeyNames":[],"groupKeys":{},"tags":"","description":"","hideIfYes":"","visibleScript":"","placeholder":"","fillIn":false,"showCopy":false,"metaOpen":false,"xrefTitle":"","xrefDetail":""}}}-->

  <!--hvy:grid:1 {}-->

   <!--hvy:image {"imageFile":"Screenshot 2026-08-27 at 12.45.35â€¯PM.png","imageAlt":"Screenshot 2026-08-27 at 12.45.35â€¯PM.png","caption":{"text":"The Greens SE (original survey)","schema":{"kind":"text","id":"","component":"text","editorOnly":false,"lock":false,"align":"center","slot":"center","css":"margin: 0.5rem 0;","sortKeys":{},"derivedSortKeyNames":[],"groupKeys":{},"tags":"","description":"","hideIfYes":"","visibleScript":"","placeholder":"","fillIn":false,"showCopy":false,"metaOpen":false,"xrefTitle":"","xrefDetail":""}}}-->

  <!--hvy:grid:2 {}-->

   <!--hvy:image {"imageFile":"Screenshot 2026-08-27 at 12.42.33â€¯PM.png","imageAlt":"Screenshot 2026-08-27 at 12.42.33â€¯PM.png","caption":{"text":"The Greens SE (6 question)","schema":{"kind":"text","id":"","component":"text","editorOnly":false,"lock":false,"align":"center","slot":"center","css":"margin: 0.5rem 0;","sortKeys":{},"derivedSortKeyNames":[],"groupKeys":{},"tags":"","description":"","hideIfYes":"","visibleScript":"","placeholder":"","fillIn":false,"showCopy":false,"metaOpen":false,"xrefTitle":"","xrefDetail":""}}}-->

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  We can also focus on the individuals who haven't heard the arguments yet:

 <!--hvy:grid {"gridColumns":3}-->
  <!--hvy:grid:0 {}-->

   <!--hvy:image {"imageFile":"Screenshot 2026-08-27 at 12.49.56â€¯PM.png","imageAlt":"Screenshot 2026-08-27 at 12.49.56â€¯PM.png","caption":{"text":"Carriage Wood","schema":{"kind":"text","id":"","component":"text","editorOnly":false,"lock":false,"align":"center","slot":"center","css":"margin: 0.5rem 0;","sortKeys":{},"derivedSortKeyNames":[],"groupKeys":{},"tags":"","description":"","hideIfYes":"","visibleScript":"","placeholder":"","fillIn":false,"showCopy":false,"metaOpen":false,"xrefTitle":"","xrefDetail":""}}}-->

  <!--hvy:grid:1 {}-->

   <!--hvy:image {"imageFile":"Screenshot 2026-08-27 at 12.51.12â€¯PM.png","imageAlt":"Screenshot 2026-08-27 at 12.51.12â€¯PM.png","caption":{"text":"The Greens SE (original survey)","schema":{"kind":"text","id":"","component":"text","editorOnly":false,"lock":false,"align":"center","slot":"center","css":"margin: 0.5rem 0;","sortKeys":{},"derivedSortKeyNames":[],"groupKeys":{},"tags":"","description":"","hideIfYes":"","visibleScript":"","placeholder":"","fillIn":false,"showCopy":false,"metaOpen":false,"xrefTitle":"","xrefDetail":""}}}-->

  <!--hvy:grid:2 {}-->

   <!--hvy:image {"imageFile":"Screenshot 2026-08-27 at 12.51.34â€¯PM.png","imageAlt":"Screenshot 2026-08-27 at 12.51.34â€¯PM.png","caption":{"text":"The Greens SE (6 question)","schema":{"kind":"text","id":"","component":"text","editorOnly":false,"lock":false,"align":"center","slot":"center","css":"margin: 0.5rem 0;","sortKeys":{},"derivedSortKeyNames":[],"groupKeys":{},"tags":"","description":"","hideIfYes":"","visibleScript":"","placeholder":"","fillIn":false,"showCopy":false,"metaOpen":false,"xrefTitle":"","xrefDetail":""}}}-->

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  This suggests the neighborhoods of Trovitsky Park, Woodside, and Lake Desire have skewed things stronger towards
  "strongly do not support," but overall, "strongly do not support" is still the predominate vote and opposition to the
  course still holds a strong majority. Throwing out the neighborhoods closest to the park because their vote is
  inconvenient is also not prudent here, as the intent is to make an informed decision. The neighborhoods closest to the
  park are also the most familiar with it, and also the most familiar with its hidden value and intangibles. They would
  also be most familiar with the arguments for / against the course.
<!--hvy: {"lock":false,"expanded":true,"highlight":false}-->
#! Survey Results

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  # Survey Results and Examples

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  Survey results with faceting and by neighborhood results, as well as examples of the three survey variants can be
  viewed at [https://PetroParkSurvey.org](https://PetroParkSurvey.org)
